      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Dürr–Høyer Minimum Finding\n",
        "\n",
        "## Problem Statement\n",
        "\n",
        "Given an **unsorted array of $N$ elements**, find the element having the minimum value.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, for the array\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=[7,2,9,4,1,6,8,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the minimum element is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{1}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A classical minimum-finding algorithm generally examines all $N$ elements, giving a time complexity of $O(N)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Durr-Hoyer Minimum Finding uses **quantum search and amplitude amplification** to find the minimum with an expected query complexity of\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{O(\\sqrt{N})}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for an unsorted set."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fv9ppVpCHddZ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Main Idea\n",
        "\n",
        "The main idea of Durr-Hoyer Minimum Finding is to maintain a **current candidate minimum** and use quantum search to look for an element smaller than that candidate.\n",
        "\n",
        "Initially, any element can be selected as the candidate.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, consider\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=[7,2,9,4,1,6,8,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the initial candidate is $7$. The algorithm searches for elements satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_i < 7\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The elements satisfying this condition are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "[2,4,1,6,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the quantum search finds $4$, the candidate is updated:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "7\\rightarrow4\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The search is then repeated for elements satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_i < 4\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the candidate may become $2$, followed by $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The basic process is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{\n",
        "\\text{Choose Candidate}\n",
        "\\rightarrow\n",
        "\\text{Search for Smaller Element}\n",
        "\\rightarrow\n",
        "\\text{Update Candidate}\n",
        "\\rightarrow\n",
        "\\text{Repeat}\n",
        "}\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4UsKIJZeH0OH"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Classical View\n",
        "\n",
        "In classical minimum finding, the array is examined element by element.\n",
        "\n",
        "For\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=[7,2,9,4,1,6,8,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "we initially assume\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{min}=7\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and compare the remaining elements with the current minimum. The candidate is updated whenever a smaller value is encountered:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "7\\rightarrow2\\rightarrow1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, classical minimum finding requires\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\boxed{O(N)}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "comparisons in the worst case.\n",
        "\n",
        "The quantum approach instead uses **amplitude amplification** to search for elements that can improve the current candidate."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rpTa_wovH3zo"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Quantum Representation\n",
        "\n",
        "The indices of the array are represented using quantum states.\n",
        "\n",
        "For $N$ elements, approximately\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\lceil\\log_2 N\\rceil\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "qubits are required to represent the indices.\n",
        "\n",
        "For\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=[7,2,9,4,1,6,8,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "there are $N=8$ elements, so\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log_2 8=3\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and three qubits are sufficient.\n",
        "\n",
        "The computational basis states represent the indices:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|000\\rangle\\rightarrow0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|001\\rangle\\rightarrow1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\vdots\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|111\\rangle\\rightarrow7\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The corresponding array value is obtained using the function\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f(i)=A_i\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The quantum search does not directly search for the numerical minimum. Instead, it searches for an **index whose value is smaller than the current candidate**."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "N9f5UvxFH6YE"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Quantum Search for a Smaller Element\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the current candidate has value $m$.\n",
        "\n",
        "We define the search condition as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f(i)<m\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "An index satisfying this condition is considered a **marked state**.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, if\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m=7\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=[7,2,9,4,1,6,8,3]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then the marked indices are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1,3,4,5,7\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "because their corresponding values are smaller than $7$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The oracle marks these states by applying a phase change. Amplitude amplification then increases the probability of measuring one of the marked indices."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "X39OvylvH_Eb"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Updating the Candidate\n",
        "\n",
        "After the quantum search, an index is measured. The corresponding array value is then compared with the current candidate.\n",
        "\n",
        "If\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_i<m\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the candidate is updated:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m\\leftarrow A_i\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, the candidate may be updated as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "7\\rightarrow4\\rightarrow2\\rightarrow1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the measured element is not smaller than the candidate, the candidate remains unchanged and the search can be repeated.\n",
        "\n",
        "This adaptive updating is the key idea of the minimum-finding procedure."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "EIW99u0zIOXQ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Updating the Candidate\n",
        "\n",
